course: dictionary
lesson: 15124
title: Sandstorm and its meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A sandstorm is a strong wind in a desert that lifts sand and carries it through the air in huge clouds. «A sandstorm blew up over the desert, and the sky turned orange.» The clues are strong wind and clouds of sand, so the sky is orange because the air is full of dust.
explain: Sandstorms are a real danger in places like the Sahara and Australia's outback. They can block out the sun, damage crops and make breathing hard. «The travellers were caught in a sandstorm and had to cover their faces with scarves.» The travellers were in danger, so the story shows that sandstorms are dangerous.
explain: Writers also use sandstorm in a figurative way, which means it is not about real sand. «When the news broke, the classroom became a sandstorm of questions.» Nobody in the room was blown away. The word means a wild, confusing rush of questions, all flying around at once.
fact: A sandstorm is a strong wind that carries clouds of sand through the air.
fact: Sandstorms happen in deserts, such as the Sahara and Australia's outback.
fact: A sandstorm can block out the sun, damage crops and make breathing hard.
fact: To stay safe in a sandstorm, go indoors, close the windows and cover your nose and mouth.
fact: In a figurative sense, a sandstorm can mean a wild, confusing rush of something, such as «a sandstorm of questions».
exercise: «A sandstorm blew up over the desert.» What is a sandstorm? | A type of sandcastle | A large caravan of camels | A gentle rain that cools the sand | *A strong wind that carries clouds of sand through the air
exercise: «The travellers were caught in a sandstorm.» What does caught in a sandstorm mean? | Sleeping in a warm tent | Eating a picnic in the shade | Taking a photo of the desert | *Stuck in a strong wind full of flying sand
exercise: What should you do if a sandstorm is coming? | *Stay indoors and close the windows | Shout loudly at the sky | Ride your bike fast to beat it | Run out into the open to watch it
test: «When the news broke, the classroom became a sandstorm of questions.» What does sandstorm mean here? | A pile of sand on the floor | *A wild, confusing rush of questions | A quiet, calm lesson | A long wait for the bell
test: Where is a sandstorm most likely to happen? | *A hot desert such as the Sahara | A snowy mountain top | A deep ocean | A busy shopping street
exam: Why are sandstorms dangerous for travellers in a desert? | *They can make breathing hard and hide the way | They make the sand cool and wet | They turn the sky into a rainbow | They help plants grow faster
